[:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/923550"} "95 from 49 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Course Length: 15,390 m" [:br {}] "Height Differential: 85 m" [:br {}] "Intermediate 1: 4.9 km" [:br {}] "Intermediate 2: 7.5 km" [:br {}] "Intermediate 3: 12.4 km" [:br {}] "Maximum Climb: 59 m" [:br {}] "Total Climbing: 564 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The defending champion and 2009 World Champion, Estonia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/98596"} "Andrus Veerpalu"] ", did not contest the event, starting only in the 50 km in Vancouver. This likely left " [:a {:href "/athletes/100078"} "Lukáš Bauer"] " (CZE) as the favorite. Bauer was leading the 2009-10 World Cup, had won the distance and overall World Cup in 2007-08, and was runner-up to Veerpalu in the 15 km at the 2009 Worlds. With skiers setting off at 30 second intervals, " [:a {:href "/athletes/118811"} "Maurice Manificat"] " (FRA) took an early lead in the competition, leading at the first two intermediate markers, and would eventually finish sixth with a time of 34:27.4. " [:a {:href "/athletes/100096"} "Pietro Piller Cottrer"] " (ITA), who had won the test event at the course some 12 months earlier, had been quoted as saying that the course was too easy, and as he skied into a lead with a time of 34:00.9, it looked as though he may win gold. However, out on the course " [:a {:href "/athletes/118753"} "Dario Cologna"] " (SUI), who had won the 2009 Tour de Ski, was the leader at the final check point and took the lead with a time of 33:36.3, a time which would easily hold up for the gold medal, giving Switzerland its first ever gold in cross-country skiing. Bauer followed home in a time of 34:12.0 to take the bronze medal, narrowly defeating " [:a {:href "/athletes/118802"} "Marcus Hellner"] " (SWE) who was always close to a medal spot on the course, but just faded at the end to finish 1.5 seconds behind Bauer. He would later win a pursuit gold in Vancouver. Finishing in 94th place was " [:a {:href "/athletes/118454"} "Roberto Carcelén"] ", the first ever Winter Olympian from Peru."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button907706", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button907706').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Intermediate 1"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Intermediate 2"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Intermediate 3"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "33"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/118753"} "Dario Cologna"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} "33:36.3"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "11:02.1 (2)"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "16:49.5 (2)"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "27:53.3 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "25"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/100096"} "Pietro Piller Cottrer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "34:00.9"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "11:05.5 (3)"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "16:54.5 (3)"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "28:07.7 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "34"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/100078"} "Lukáš Bauer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CZE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags